      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine the number of ways to arrange 3 math books and 5 English books on a shelf such that all the math books stay together and all the English books stay together, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Treat the math books as a single \"block\" and the English books as another \"block\":**\n   Since all the math books must stay together and all the English books must stay together, we can consider each group as a single unit or \"block\". So, we have two blocks: one block of math books and one block of English books.\n\n2. **Arrange the blocks:**\n   There are 2 blocks, and we can arrange these 2 blocks in \\(2!\\) (2 factorial) ways. Since:\n   \\[\n   2! = 2 \\times 1 = 2\n   \\]\n   Therefore, there are 2 ways to arrange the blocks.\n\n3. **Arrange the books within each block:**\n   - There are 3 math books, and they can be arranged among themselves in \\(3!\\) (3 factorial) ways. Since:\n   \\[\n   3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n   \\]\n   Therefore, there are 6 ways to arrange the math books.\n   - There are 5 English books, and they can be arranged among themselves in \\(5!\\) (5 factorial) ways. Since:\n   \\[\n   5! = 5 \\times 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 120\n   \\]\n   Therefore, there are 120 ways to arrange the English books.\n\n4. **Combine the arrangements:**\n   To find the total number of ways to arrange the books, we multiply the number of ways to arrange the blocks by the number of ways to arrange the books within each block:\n   \\[\n   2! \\times 3! \\times 5! = 2 \\times 6 \\times 120\n   \\]\n   Now, we calculate the product:\n   \\[\n   2 \\times 6 = 12\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   12 \\times 120 = 1440\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of ways to arrange the books is \\(\\boxed{1440}\\).",
