      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to consider the two groups of books separately and then combine their arrangements.\n\n1. **Treat the math books as a single unit**:\n   Since all the math books must stay together, we can treat the 3 math books as a single \"super book\". This reduces the problem to arranging 2 units: the \"super book\" of math books and the 5 English books.\n\n2. **Arrange the 2 units**:\n   The number of ways to arrange 2 units is given by the factorial of the number of units. Here, we have 2 units, so the number of arrangements is:\n   \\[\n   2! = 2 \\times 1 = 2\n   \\]\n   The two units can be arranged as \"math books\" followed by \"English books\" or \"English books\" followed by \"math books\".\n\n3. **Arrange the books within each unit**:\n   - The 3 math books can be arranged among themselves in:\n     \\[\n     3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n     \\]\n   - The 5 English books can be arranged among themselves in:\n     \\[\n     5! = 5 \\times 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 120\n     \\]\n\n4. **Combine the arrangements**:\n   The total number of ways to arrange the books is the product of the number of ways to arrange the 2 units and the number of ways to arrange the books within each unit:\n   \\[\n   2! \\times 3! \\times 5! = 2 \\times 6 \\times 120 = 1440\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to arrange the 3 math books and 5 English books on a shelf, with all math books together and all English books together, is \\(\\boxed{1440}\\).",
