      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the problem of arranging 3 math books and 5 English books on a shelf such that all math books are together and all English books are together, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Treat each group of books as a single \"super book\":**\n   - We have 3 math books that must stay together. We can treat these 3 math books as one \"super book\".\n   - We also have 5 English books that must stay together. We can treat these 5 English books as one \"super book\".\n\n2. **Form a new \"super shelf\":**\n   - Now, we have 2 \"super books\": one containing the 3 math books and one containing the 5 English books.\n   - We need to arrange these 2 \"super books\" on the shelf.\n\n3. **Arrange the \"super books\":**\n   - There are 2 \"super books\", and we can arrange them in \\(2!\\) (2 factorial) ways.\n   \\[\n   2! = 2 \\times 1 = 2\n   \\]\n   So, there are 2 ways to arrange the 2 \"super books\".\n\n4. **Arrange the books within each \"super book\":**\n   - The 3 math books can be arranged among themselves in \\(3!\\) (3 factorial) ways.\n   \\[\n   3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n   \\]\n   So, there are 6 ways to arrange the 3 math books within their \"super book\".\n\n   Similarly, the 5 English books can be arranged among themselves in \\(5!\\) (5 factorial) ways.\n   \\[\n   5! = 5 \\times 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 120\n   \\]\n   So, there are 120 ways to arrange the 5 English books within their \"super book\".\n\n5. **Combine the arrangements:**\n   - The total number of ways to arrange the books is the product of the number of ways to arrange the \"super books\" and the number of ways to arrange the books within each \"super book\".\n   \\[\n   2! \\times 3! \\times 5! = 2 \\times 6 \\times 120 = 1440\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of ways to arrange the 3 math books and 5 English books on the shelf, with all math books together and all English books together, is \\(\\boxed{1440}\\).",
