      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the number of ways to arrange 3 math books and 5 English books on a shelf such that all the math books must stay together and all the English books must also stay together.\n\nWe can treat the 3 math books as a single \"super book\" and the 5 English books as another \"super book\". This reduces the problem to arranging 2 \"super books\" on the shelf.\n\nFirst, we calculate the number of ways to arrange these 2 \"super books\". Since there are only 2 \"super books\", there is only 1 way to arrange them:\n\n\\[\n1 \\text{ (way to arrange the 2 \"super books\")}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to determine the number of ways to arrange the 3 math books within their \"super book\" and the 5 English books within their \"super book\". The 3 math books can be arranged among themselves in:\n\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6 \\text{ ways}\n\\]\n\nThe 5 English books can be arranged among themselves in:\n\n\\[\n5! = 5 \\times 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 120 \\text{ ways}\n\\]\n\nTo find the total number of ways to arrange the books, we multiply the number of ways to arrange the \"super books\" by the number of ways to arrange the math books and the English books within their respective \"super books\":\n\n\\[\n1 \\times 3! \\times 5! = 1 \\times 6 \\times 120 = 720\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of ways to arrange the 3 math books and 5 English books on the shelf is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{720}\n\\]",
